A recommendation is a category-B-recommendation and is an evidence-type-4-recommendation.
If a patient receives an opioid-therapy then every clinician should carefully weigh a benefit against a risk for the patient.
If a clinician changes an opioid-dosage for a patient that receives an opioid-therapy then the clinician should carefully change the opioid-dosage.
If a benefit outweighs a risk then every clinician should closely work with a patient and should optimize a nonopioid-therapy and should continue an opioid-therapy.
If a benefit does not outweigh a risk then every clinician should optimize a different-therapy and should closely work with a patient and should gradually taper an opioid-dosage to a lower-opioid-dosage.
If a benefit does not outweigh a risk and a patient takes an opioid and an opioid-discontinuation is warranted for the patient then every clinician should appropriately taper the opioid and should appropriately discontinue the opioid.
Every clinician that does not provably detect a life-threatening-warning should not abruptly discontinue an opioid and should not rapidly reduce an opioid-dosage from a higher-dosage.
